 "source": "HARMAN technology Limited, PANF PLUS Technical Information, document B26 (no printed issue date; PDF created 5 May 2026); curves traced from the PDF's raster chart images (spectral page 1, characteristic curve page 4)",
 "notes": "Approximate digitization; not supplied or endorsed by HARMAN technology. Both charts are embedded raster images (no vector curve paths); curves were traced at the images' native resolution with a centreline follower after thin grid and frame lines were removed by a 3x3 grey opening, axes calibrated on the printed grid lines and tick marks. Characteristic curve (page 4): the datasheet prints a single curve (ILFORD ILFOTEC HC (1+31), 4 min at 20 C/68 F, intermittent agitation (roll film; stated to be representative of the 35mm film format)); no other developer or time is charted, so all_curves holds only this curve. x is the chart's own 'Relative log exposure' axis (labels 1-4, arbitrary origin), not log H in lux-seconds; density axis labelled 1.0 and 2.0, zero taken at the bottom grid line by the equal grid spacing; tracing noise in the flat toe was removed with a running maximum so density is non-decreasing. log_h_ref: none. The chart carries no 18% grey, speed-point or normal-exposure mark, and the text gives only the film speed (ISO 50/18 to daylight, measured with ILFORD ID-11 at 20 C/68 F with intermittent agitation in a spiral tank; best results stated at EI 50/18 (exposure rating text, page 1)), so no reference exposure is placed on the relative axis. Spectral sensitivity (page 1): 'Wedge spectrogram to tungsten light (2850K)'; the vertical axis is labelled 'Sensitivity' with only two ticks, 0.5 and 1.0, and no statement whether it is linear or logarithmic; values are read on that tick scale as charted (linear between and beyond the ticks), so the curve ends where it meets the chart's bottom frame, which lies at -0.01 on that scale. No granularity or MTF data are published. Not recorded (no field in this schema): processing-temperature charts (page 4), reciprocity graph (page 2, Ta = Tm^1.33).",
